DDL (Data Definition Language) commands are used to define and modify the structure of database objects. All DDL commands end with a semicolon (;).

1. CREATE — Used to create new databases or tables.
Syntax: CREATE database databasename;
Example: mysql>create database studentDB;
Also used to create tables: CREATE TABLE tablename (column definitions);

2. ALTER — Modifies the existing structure of a database or table. Used to add, delete, or modify columns in an existing table.
Syntax: ALTER TABLE tablename ADD/DROP/MODIFY column_definition;

3. DROP — Deletes an entire database or table permanently.
Syntax: DROP database databasename;
Example: mysql>DROP database studentDB;
WARNING: Once dropped, all data is permanently lost.

4. RENAME — Used to rename an existing database object.
Syntax: RENAME TABLE old_name TO new_name;

5. TRUNCATE — Deletes ALL records from a table but keeps the table structure intact. Faster than DELETE for removing all rows.
Syntax: TRUNCATE TABLE tablename;

Key difference: DROP removes the entire table structure, TRUNCATE removes only the data (structure remains).
